From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 04:09:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 019/342] arm64: invalidate the whole instruction cache when it
 is VIPT

arch_cpu_sync_icache() is called by the translation map with the
physical map alias of a page (KERNEL_PMAP_BASE + pa), and invalidated
the instruction cache by that address. The Cortex-A53's L1 instruction
cache is VIPT with 16 KiB ways (CTR_EL0 0x84448004), so it aliases:
lines cached under the address the code runs at survived, and the CPU
could execute a previous mapping's instructions.

On the ODROID-C2 this showed as intermittent boot failures: a PC
alignment fault in xhci's supports_device(), where the CPU executed
"blr x4" (from ehci/ohci/uhci, loaded at the same address just before)
while memory held the correct "movi", and desktop hangs. QEMU models no
caches, so it never happens there.

When CTR_EL0.L1Ip is not PIPT, invalidate the whole instruction cache
on all CPUs (ic ialluis), as Linux does for aliasing instruction
caches. Also fix the instruction cache line size, which was computed
from "ctr_el0 << 0xF" and came out as 4 bytes, and print CTR_EL0 at
boot.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_cpu.cpp | 33 +++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_cpu.cpp b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_cpu.cpp
index 8f622310d0..4763a0a78d 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_cpu.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/arch_cpu.cpp
@@ -54,6 +54,11 @@ arch_cpu_init(kernel_args *args)
 {
 	sPsciConduit = args->arch_args.psci_conduit;
 
+	uint64_t ctr_el0;
+	asm volatile ("mrs\t%0, ctr_el0":"=r" (ctr_el0));
+	dprintf("arm64: CTR_EL0 %#" B_PRIx64 ", instruction cache %s\n", ctr_el0,
+		((ctr_el0 >> 14) & 3) == 3 ? "PIPT" : "VIPT (synced by invalidating all)");
+
 	for (uint32 i = 0; i < args->num_cpus; i++) {
 		cpu_ent* cpu = &gCPU[i];
 
@@ -109,7 +114,7 @@ arch_cpu_sync_icache(void *address, size_t len)
 	uint64_t ctr_el0 = 0;
 	asm volatile ("mrs\t%0, ctr_el0":"=r" (ctr_el0));
 
-	uint64_t icache_line_size = 4 << (ctr_el0 << 0xF);
+	uint64_t icache_line_size = 4 << (ctr_el0 & 0xF);
 	uint64_t dcache_line_size = 4 << ((ctr_el0 >> 16) & 0xF);
 	uint64_t addr = (uint64_t)address;
 	uint64_t end = addr + len;
@@ -119,14 +124,26 @@ arch_cpu_sync_icache(void *address, size_t len)
 		asm volatile ("dc cvau, %0" : : "r"(address_dcache) : "memory");
 	}
 
-	asm("dsb ish");
-
-	for (uint64_t address_icache = ROUNDDOWN(addr, icache_line_size);
-         address_icache < end; address_icache += icache_line_size) {
-		asm volatile ("ic ivau, %0" : : "r"(address_icache) : "memory");
+	asm volatile("dsb ish" : : : "memory");
+
+	// The translation map passes the physical map alias of a page here, not
+	// the address the code runs at. In a VIPT instruction cache
+	// (CTR_EL0.L1Ip != PIPT; the Cortex-A53's is VIPT with 16 KiB ways)
+	// invalidating by that alias misses lines cached under the code's own
+	// address, and the CPU can go on executing a previous mapping's
+	// instructions (seen with kernel add-ons loaded at the address of one
+	// just unloaded). Invalidate the whole instruction cache then, on all
+	// CPUs, as Linux does for aliasing instruction caches.
+	if (((ctr_el0 >> 14) & 3) != 3) {
+		asm volatile("ic ialluis" : : : "memory");
+	} else {
+		for (uint64_t address_icache = ROUNDDOWN(addr, icache_line_size);
+				address_icache < end; address_icache += icache_line_size) {
+			asm volatile ("ic ivau, %0" : : "r"(address_icache) : "memory");
+		}
 	}
-	asm("dsb ish");
-	asm("isb");
+	asm volatile("dsb ish" : : : "memory");
+	asm volatile("isb" : : : "memory");
 }
 
 
